+### Fixed (the desktop app waits for the discard question, 2026-11-05)
+
+- **The desktop app asks before throwing away unsaved changes.** In the Windows, macOS and Linux app, File > Open and File > New replaced a model that had unsaved changes at once, and "Discard the unsaved changes to this model?" was never seen. The dialog plugin gives the desktop's `window.confirm` back as a Promise (a yes whatever the answer, to code that does not wait), and the app did not wait; the shell had also never granted the plugin's message permission, so the dialog could not have shown. The question now goes through the dialog plugin on the desktop (`askYesNo` in `src/io/files.ts`, with the app's name on the dialog) and is awaited wherever it is asked: Open, New, and an empty SketchUp file opening as a new model. The browser build asks as before. `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json` grants `dialog:allow-message`. Found on Linux (the 0.1.0-alpha.4 .deb and AppImage, and `main`) by scripting the app under Xvfb.
+- Tests: vitest (`src/io/files.test.ts`: the browser asks with window.confirm; the desktop asks through the plugin and waits for the answer); `e2e/files.spec.ts` stands in the desktop's asynchronous confirm and checks that Open and New over unsaved changes wait for No and keep the model, and go ahead on Yes.
+
 ### Fixed (lane 27, 2026-11-01)
 
 - **A SketchUp import's curve note says what the model holds** ([#17](https://github.com/sawhorse3d/sawhorse/issues/17)). openskp's `gondola_v20.skp` said "16 curves came in as curves (8 arcs, 8 polylines)" while the model held 24 polylines and no arc: the note counted a component's curves once, for its definition, in the frame its items are built in, and its arcs sit in components placed with an uneven scale, which turns each copy's arcs into polylines only where it lands. The note now counts every placed copy as it lands, as the curves view does (the gondola: "24 curves came in as curves" and "16 circles or arcs in groups or components scaled unevenly came in as polylines"), and an uneven scale on a component's placement is said with a group's: "N circles or arcs in groups or components scaled unevenly came in as polylines". Five of the 35 real files tried said a different number than their curves view (Corynth's cupboard said 30 curves, 6 circles and 24 arcs, for a model holding 12 circles and 48 arcs); none does now.
